  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 124 PM MDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nCurrent observations show areas of fog over parts of the northeast \nCO plains. A Dense Fog Advisory is currently in effect for Sedgwick \nand Phillips counties, where visibilities have dropped below 1/2 \nmile. The Dense Fog Advisory may have to be extended later tonight \nas the fog continues to expand west, with patchy fog reaching as far \nwest as Greeley. Subsidence and a patch of mid level clouds should \nlimit fog potential over the Denver metro. We expect fog to diminish \nby mid morning due to daytime heating, with the South Platte River \nvalley likely clearing last.\n\nCross-barrier (west-southwest) flow will make Wednesday quite a \nbreezy day, particularly early in the day as mountain wave and \ndownsloping enhancement occurs. Wind gusts up to 35 mph will be \npossible at the high elevations, with gusts up 55 mph possible above \n12,000 ft MSL. Temperatures will run above average for the plains \nand Denver metro, with highs reaching the upper 60s and low 70s. If \nan opaque mountain wave cloud is able to persist into the afternoon, \nas was seen on Monday, temperatures could stay a couple of degrees \ncooler for areas directly under the cloud cover. Flow aloft weakens \nand turns southwesterly on Thursday. Winds should remain light due \nto the weakening pressure gradient, and this should be a sunny fall \nday with temperatures only slightly above normal for the time of \nyear.\n\nMoisture from the remnants of Tropical Storm Vance will be advected \nnorth into our region on Friday. However, the bulk of the moisture \nshould remain well to our south and east, mainly over the Texas \nPanhandle and central Plains. We have kept some 15-30% PoPs over the \neasternmost counties of our CWA, where some light rain seems \nreasonable late Friday evening. For the rest of our area, an \nincrease in high clouds may be all that transpires as a result of \nthis first plume of moisture.\n\nMoisture remains elevated on Saturday, with precipitable water (PW) \nvalues generally between the 75th and 90th percentile of climatology \nover much of the state. A shortwave trough should bring enough QG \nascent for a few sprinkles (15-25% chance) over the eastern CO \nplains, and rain/snow showers over the mountains (30-50% chance) in \nthe afternoon.\n\nThe synoptic pattern becomes more complex on Sunday as an upper \nlevel trough and a subtropical jet approach the Four Corners. The \nsubtropical jet is set to channel impressive Pacific moisture to \nparts of Arizona and New Mexico, akin to an atmospheric river. At \nthis time, the bulk of the moisture plume appears to stay well to \nour south once again. Southwest flow aloft may still bring \nanomalous moisture to north-central CO, although this regime would\nprimarily favor downsloping, which is not ideal for meaningful \nprecipitation over the mountains of our CWA or the Denver area. \nNevertheless, cloud cover and at least some light rain seems \nincreasingly likely for Sunday, with light snow above 10,000 ft \nMSL. \n\nA strong cold front is set to bring below average temperatures on\nMonday, with model guidance suggesting quite a bit of spread in \nsolutions. With lingering moisture over the area, some mountain \nsnow accumulations are becoming increasingly likely during the day\non Monday. While a few deterministic and ensemble members do \nhint at least some dustings or light snow accumulations over the \nPalmer Divide and parts of the Denver metro, ensemble means \nsuggest temperatures that would not be supportive of snow \n(40s-50s). This will be a system to watch as snow levels are\nlikely to reach the foothills.",
